const e=[{id:"l-formation",moduleId:"m06-contract",title:"Formation: the moment a contract exists",minutes:25,summary:"Section 4 of the Contracts Act fixes a different moment for each kind of message, and section 5 decides the race between an acceptance and a withdrawal.",plants:[],prepares:["ps-formation-01"],reading:[{bookId:"cheong-contract-law-malaysia",where:"the chapters on offer and acceptance"},{bookId:"sinnadurai-law-of-contract",where:"for the fuller treatment and the case law"}],source:"Contracts Act 1950 (Act 136) ss 2(a), 4, 5, 6; Macon Works & Trading Sdn Bhd v Phang Hon Chin [1976] 2 MLJ 177.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Read ss 4 and 5 in a current reprint of Act 136 and check the subsection lettering against your text before quoting it. Confirm the Macon Works citation.",plate:"letters",plateCaption:"Two letters crossing. Section 4 fixes a different moment for each of them, and the race is decided by which moment applies.",readMinutes:11,sections:[{id:"sec-l-formation-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A room, a message, and a second tenant — whether there is a contract turns on one moment.",h:"The moment that decides it",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Raju advertises a room in Cheras at RM450 a month. Mei Ling messages him on Tuesday to say that she will take it. On Wednesday he tells her the room has gone to somebody else."},{t:"p",text:"Whether Mei Ling has a contract depends on the exact moment one came into being, if it ever did. Malaysian law fixes that moment by statute, and the statute is precise about it."}]},{id:"sec-l-formation-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Malaysian contract law is a statute, and it does not always match the English cases.",h:"A statute, not the common law",body:[{t:"p",text:"Malaysian contract law is written down in a statute. The Contracts Act 1950 sets out the rules of formation, and those rules do not always match the English ones that a common law textbook will give you."},{t:"p",text:"Copying English contract law across without checking the Act is the most reliable way to get a Malaysian contract question wrong. Formation is the clearest example, because the Act writes down the posting rules in a form that produces different answers from the English cases."}]},{id:"sec-l-formation-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Section 2(a) defines a proposal; acceptance turns it into a promise.",h:"Proposal and acceptance",body:[{t:"rule",text:"Section 2(a): when one person signifies to another his willingness to do or to abstain from doing anything, with a view to obtaining the assent of that other, he is said to make a proposal.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 2(a)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: a proposal is telling somebody that you are willing to do something, in order to get their agreement to it."},{t:"p",text:"The Act says **proposal** where an English book says offer, and **promise** where it says acceptance. The ideas are the same, and the sensible course is to use the vocabulary of the statute you are working in."},{t:"p",text:"A proposal has to be told apart from an **invitation to treat**, which is a signal that you are open to receiving proposals rather than a proposal itself. Goods displayed with a price, a newspaper advertisement and a notice of an auction are the standard examples. That distinction comes from the case law rather than from the Act itself."},{t:"example",text:"Encik Raju's advertisement for the room is an invitation to treat, not a proposal. Mei Ling's message saying that she will take it is the proposal. Raju has to accept that proposal before either of them is bound to anything."}]},{id:"sec-l-formation-3",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Section 4 fixes a different moment for each kind of message, and delivery is not knowledge.",anchor:"fig-l-formation-0",h:"When a message counts",body:[{t:"p",text:"Section 4 is the provision that decides most formation problems. The point to notice is that it fixes a different moment for each kind of message, and a different moment again depending on which side is relying on it. There is no single rule here to reach for."},{t:"rule",text:"Section 4(1): communication of a proposal is complete when it comes to the knowledge of the person to whom it is made.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 4(1)"},{t:"predict",prompt:"Mei Ling posts her acceptance on Monday. It reaches Encik Raju on Thursday, and he reads it that evening. Before you read on — from which day is Raju bound?",options:["Thursday, when he read it and knew about it","Neither — a letter binds nobody until both sides confirm","Monday, when she put the letter into the post"],answer:2,reveal:"Monday. It feels wrong that a man can be bound by a letter he has not opened, and that feeling is not silly. The Act is lopsided here on purpose. Sending is enough against the proposer, and only against him. Mei Ling herself is not bound until Thursday, when Raju knows. One posting, two different moments."},{t:"rule",text:"Section 4(2): communication of an acceptance is complete — (a) as against the proposer, when it is put in a course of transmission to him, so as to be out of the power of the acceptor; and (b) as against the acceptor, when it comes to the knowledge of the proposer.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 4(2)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: sending the acceptance binds the proposer straight away, but it does not bind the acceptor until the proposer knows about it."},{t:"p",text:"A **revocation** is a withdrawal — the proposer taking his offer back before it has been accepted. Section 4(3) gives a revocation the same two-sided treatment as an acceptance."},{t:"rule",text:"Section 4(3): communication of a revocation is complete — (a) as against the person who makes it, when it is put into a course of transmission; and (b) as against the person to whom it is made, when it comes to his knowledge.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 4(3)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: sending a withdrawal commits the sender straight away, but it does nothing at all to the other side until that side actually knows of it."},{t:"p",text:"Read those provisions together and a pattern appears. Sending is enough to bind the person the message is aimed at in exactly one case: an acceptance, as against the proposer. Everywhere else the Act asks for knowledge, and that lopsidedness is deliberate."},{t:"table",caption:"The four moments section 4 fixes",columns:[{label:"Communication"},{label:"Complete as against the maker"},{label:"Complete as against the other party"}],rows:[["Proposal — s 4(1)","—","When it comes to that person's knowledge"],["Acceptance — s 4(2)","When put into a course of transmission, out of the acceptor's power","When it comes to the proposer's knowledge"],["Revocation — s 4(3)","When put into a course of transmission","When it comes to that person's knowledge"]],note:"The trap is the last cell. **Delivery is not knowledge** — an unopened letter on a desk has been delivered and not communicated, and a problem that mentions one is testing exactly that.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 4"}]},{id:"sec-l-formation-4",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A proposal can be revoked until acceptance is complete against the proposer — which is why the moment matters.",h:"The race",body:[{t:"rule",text:"Section 5(1): a proposal may be revoked at any time before the communication of its acceptance is complete as against the proposer, but not afterwards.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 5(1)"},{t:"p",text:"Put section 5(1) beside section 4(2)(a) and the deadline becomes exact. The proposer may take his offer back until the moment the acceptance is sent, and not one minute after that."},{t:"p",text:"Put it beside section 4(3)(b) and the other half of the race appears. His withdrawal does not touch the acceptor until the acceptor actually knows about it."},{t:"example",text:"Raju posts an offer to Mei Ling on 3 March. On 10 March he changes his mind and posts a letter withdrawing it. That letter is delivered to Mei Ling's office on the afternoon of 12 March, but she does not open it until 14 March. She had already posted her acceptance at nine in the morning on 12 March. That acceptance bound Raju the moment it went into the postbox. The withdrawal reached Mei Ling only on 14 March, when she read it, so it was too late and there is a contract."},{t:"caution",text:"Delivery is not the same thing as knowledge. Section 4(3)(b) asks for knowledge, and a letter lying unopened on a desk has not been communicated to anybody at all. This is the step that most answers skip over."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Raju's letter withdrawing his offer is delivered to Mei Ling's office on Monday. She opens it on Wednesday. When is the withdrawal complete against her?",options:["Monday — the letter was in her hands","The day Raju posted it","Wednesday — that is when she knew of it"],answer:2,why:"Section 4(3)(b) asks for knowledge, and an unopened letter on a desk is not knowledge. The first answer is the step most answers skip: delivery is not communication. The third is the right rule read off the wrong half of the subsection. Posting binds Raju, and it does nothing to Mei Ling."},{t:"p",text:"Section 6 lists the ways a proposal comes to an end. They are notice of revocation, lapse of the time allowed, and failure to fulfil a condition. A proposal also ends on the death or mental disorder of the proposer, where the acceptor knows of it before accepting."}]},{id:"sec-l-formation-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A promise to hold an offer open binds nobody without consideration.",h:"An offer held open",body:[{t:"p",text:"Raju tells Mei Ling that the room is hers if she decides within fourteen days. Three days later he lets it to somebody else instead. Was he allowed to do that?"},{t:"p",text:"He was, unless she gave him something for the promise to wait. A promise to keep an offer open is itself a promise, and a promise that is not paid for does not bind. What each side gives in return for the other's promise is called **consideration**."},{t:"rule",text:"A promise to keep an offer open for a stated period is not binding unless supported by consideration; the proposer may revoke within the period.",source:"Macon Works & Trading Sdn Bhd v Phang Hon Chin [1976] 2 MLJ 177"},{t:"caution",text:"It is easy to over-apply this rule. All it means is that the proposer *could* have revoked in time, not that he actually did so. Check the timing under sections 4 and 5 before concluding either way."}]},{id:"sec-l-formation-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Two letters crossing in the post, and both sides arguing something wrong.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"The problem question is two letters crossing in the post, with both sides advancing an argument that is wrong."},{t:"p",text:"Working it properly means applying a different subsection to each message, rather than reaching for one rule and using it everywhere."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qfm1",q:"When is communication of a proposal complete?",options:["When it is posted","When it is signed","When it reaches the knowledge of the person it is made to","When it is written"],answer:2,why:"Section 4(1). Each kind of message has its own moment, so read the right subsection.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 4(1)"},{id:"qfm2",q:"When is an acceptance communicated, as against the proposer?",options:["When it is put in a course of transmission and is out of the acceptor's power","When it reaches the proposer","When the proposer reads it","When the acceptor makes up her mind"],answer:0,why:"Section 4(2)(a). As against the acceptor it is complete only when it comes to the proposer's knowledge.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 4(2)"},{id:"qfm3",q:"Until when may a proposal be taken back?",options:["Until the acceptance is received","At any time before the contract is performed","Until the date the offer says it expires","Until communication of the acceptance is complete as against the proposer"],answer:3,why:"Section 5(1), which is why the moment fixed by section 4(2)(a) decides the race.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 5(1)"},{id:"qfm4",q:"A letter withdrawing an offer sits unopened on the offeree's desk. Has it been communicated to her?",options:["Yes, on delivery","Yes, on posting","No — section 4(3)(b) asks for knowledge","Only if it was sent by registered post"],answer:2,why:"Delivery is not knowledge, and this is the step most answers get wrong.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 4(3)(b)"},{id:"qfm5",q:"A proposer promises to keep an offer open for fourteen days. Is that promise binding?",options:["Not unless something was given for it","Yes, always","Yes, if it is in writing","Yes, if the other side relied on it"],answer:0,why:"It means he could have revoked in time, not that he did. Check the timing under sections 4 and 5 first.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 ss 4, 5 and 26"}]},{id:"l-consideration",moduleId:"m06-contract",title:"Consideration under a statute, not the common law",minutes:25,summary:"Section 2(d) differs from English law in two ways that decide cases, and section 26 rescues three promises that would otherwise fail.",plants:[],prepares:["ps-consideration-01"],reading:[{bookId:"cheong-contract-law-malaysia",where:"the chapter on consideration"},{bookId:"sinnadurai-law-of-contract",where:"on s 26 and the exceptions in detail"}],source:"Contracts Act 1950 (Act 136) ss 2(d), 26 and Explanation 2; Kepong Prospecting Ltd v Schmidt [1968] 1 MLJ 170 (PC).",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Read s 26 and its exceptions in a current reprint of Act 136 — the registration requirement in (a) and the exact wording of (c) are what answers turn on. Read Kepong Prospecting itself rather than a summary, since it is cited for both consideration and privity and the two are easy to run together.",plate:"exchange",plateCaption:"The exchange, and the third figure at the table — under section 2(d) consideration may move from a person who is not the promisee.",readMinutes:11,glossaryDense:"The section 26 rule block recites the statute's three saved promises nearly verbatim, and a statutory list is made of terms of art. Fifteen marks in ninety-seven words is the provision's density, not the glossary's.",sections:[{id:"sec-l-consideration-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A promise to give money, freely made and then withdrawn, is usually unenforceable — and that surprises people.",h:"Why a bare promise fails",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Rohana tells her nephew that she will give him RM3,000 because she is proud of him. Two months later she changes her mind and says nothing more about it. Can he sue her for the money?"},{t:"predict",prompt:"Puan Rohana promised her nephew RM3,000 because she was proud of him. Then she changed her mind. Can he sue her for it?",options:["Yes — she said it plainly and she meant it","Only if she had put it in writing","No — he gave her nothing in return"],answer:2,reveal:"The second one. If you picked the first, you are holding the ordinary moral view of a promise, and the law is about to part company with it. It asks a narrower question: what did the other side give? Writing alone will not rescue a bare promise, though writing matters later in this lesson."},{t:"p",text:"Usually he cannot. A promise on its own is not a contract, and Malaysian law asks what the other side gave in return for it. What the other side gives is called **consideration**, and it is the price of a promise."},{t:"p",text:"This is the topic where borrowing English law does the most damage. The English rules are famous, and the Malaysian definition is statutory and different. Two of those differences decide ordinary cases, and both of them sit in a single sentence of the Act."}]},{id:"sec-l-consideration-1",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Section 2(d) is wider than English law: past consideration counts, and it may move from a third party.",h:"The definition",body:[{t:"rule",text:"Section 2(d): when, at the desire of the promisor, the promisee or any other person has done or abstained from doing, or does or abstains from doing, or promises to do or to abstain from doing, something, such act or abstinence or promise is called a consideration for the promise.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 2(d)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: consideration is whatever the other side does, does not do, or promises, because you asked for it. It may be something already finished, and it may come from a third person."},{t:"p",text:"Two phrases in that section should stand out, because each of them departs from English law."},{t:"list",items:["“Has done or abstained from doing” — an act already finished can count. The English rule that past consideration is no consideration does not apply here.","“The promisee or any other person” — the consideration need not come from the person the promise was made to, because a stranger to the promise may supply it."]},{t:"p",text:"Kepong Prospecting is the case usually cited for the first point. The Privy Council there treated services rendered before the agreement as good consideration under section 2(d)."},{t:"rule",text:"Past consideration is good consideration in Malaysia, and consideration need not move from the promisee.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 2(d); Kepong Prospecting Ltd v Schmidt [1968] 1 MLJ 170 (PC)"},{t:"example",text:"In June Pak Samad asks Ah Meng to repair his fence, and says nothing at all about paying him. Ah Meng goes ahead and does the work. In July Pak Samad promises him RM300 for it. The work was finished before the promise, but it was done at Pak Samad's request, so under section 2(d) it can be good consideration."},{t:"caution",text:"This does not abolish the doctrine of privity. **Privity of contract** means that only a party to a contract can sue on it. Consideration may come from a stranger, but a stranger still cannot bring the claim. Keep the two rules apart, because Kepong Prospecting gets cited for both and they are easily run together."},{t:"p",text:"The words “at the desire of the promisor” remain a real requirement. An act nobody asked for, done out of ordinary kindness, is not obviously consideration even though it is past. That is the point at which section 26 becomes the safer argument to run."}]},{id:"sec-l-consideration-2",readMinutes:5,keypoint:"Section 26 voids a bare promise, then saves three of them — and limb (a) needs writing AND registration.",anchor:"fig-l-consideration-0",h:"Section 26 and its three saves",body:[{t:"rule",text:"Section 26: an agreement made without consideration is void, unless — (a) it is in writing and registered, and made on account of natural love and affection between parties standing in a near relation to each other; (b) it is a promise to compensate, wholly or in part, a person who has already voluntarily done something for the promisor; or (c) it is a promise, in writing and signed by the person to be charged, to pay wholly or in part a debt of which the creditor might have enforced payment but for the law of limitation.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 26"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: no consideration, no contract — with three escapes, and each escape has a form to follow."},{t:"list",items:["(a) It is in writing and registered, and made on account of natural love and affection between parties standing in a near relation to each other.","(b) It is a promise to compensate, wholly or in part, a person who has already voluntarily done something for the promisor.","(c) It is a promise in writing, signed by the person to be charged, to pay a debt the creditor could have enforced but for the law of limitation."]},{t:"p",text:"**Limitation** is the period within which an action must be brought. Once that period has run out the debt is still owed, but the courts will no longer enforce it."},{t:"p",text:"Each exception carries its own formality, and it is usually the formality rather than the substance that decides the case."},{t:"list",items:["Limb (a) needs writing *and* registration, which is a demanding pair. Writing on its own is not enough, and this is the limb most often stated only halfway.","Limb (b) needs the earlier act to have been voluntary and done for the promisor. It is the safe route for the good turn that nobody asked for.","Limb (c) needs writing signed by the person who is to pay, and a debt that is time-barred rather than merely unpaid."]},{t:"example",text:"Ah Meng finds Puan Rohana's cat and brings it home, without being asked to. A week later she signs a note promising him RM500 for his trouble. Section 2(d) is awkward here, because nobody had asked him to do anything at all. Section 26(b) fits squarely, since it covers a promise to compensate a person who has already voluntarily done something for the promisor. That is the limb to argue first."},{t:"example",text:"A father promises his daughter RM20,000 out of natural love and affection when she finishes her degree. They are plainly in a near relation, and the love and affection is expressly the reason. But nothing was put in writing and nothing was registered, so section 26(a) is not satisfied and the promise is void."},{t:"caution",text:"Failing that second promise for want of an intention to create legal relations gives the right outcome for the wrong reason. The Act hands you a clear formality, so reach for the provision rather than for a general idea."},{t:"table",caption:"Section 26: the three saved promises",columns:[{label:"Limb"},{label:"What it requires"},{label:"The trap"}],rows:[["26(a)","Writing **and** registration; natural love and affection; parties in a near relation","Writing alone is not enough — this is the limb most often stated incompletely"],["26(b)","A promise to compensate a past act that was voluntary and done for the promisor","The act must have been voluntary, and done *for the promisor*"],["26(c)","A written, signed promise to pay a debt barred by limitation","The debt must actually be time-barred, not merely unpaid"]],source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 26"}]},{id:"sec-l-consideration-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Inadequate consideration does not void an agreement, though it may bear on whether consent was free.",h:"Is the price too small?",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Rohana sells her motorbike to her cousin for RM100 when the bike is plainly worth RM4,000. Is the sale void because the price is absurd?"},{t:"rule",text:"Explanation 2 to s 26: an agreement is not void merely because the consideration is inadequate, but inadequacy may be taken into account in determining whether the promisor's consent was freely given.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 26, Explanation 2"},{t:"p",text:"So the courts do not police whether a bargain was a good one. A wildly one-sided bargain is, however, evidence that something went wrong in the making of it — pressure, perhaps, or a misunderstanding."}]},{id:"sec-l-consideration-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Three promises, and a colleague who has advised that all three fail out of an English textbook.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"The problem question gives you three promises and a junior colleague who has advised that all three of them fail. His grounds are taken straight out of an English textbook."},{t:"p",text:"Two of the three promises actually succeed. The exercise is noticing which of his propositions is English law that the Malaysian Act has displaced."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qcs1",q:"How does section 2(d) differ from English law?",options:["Consideration must be adequate","Past consideration is good, and consideration need not come from the promisee","Consideration must be in writing","Consideration must come from the promisee alone"],answer:1,why:"Both differences decide cases, and both come straight from the words of the section.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 2(d)"},{id:"qcs2",q:"Does section 2(d) mean a stranger to a contract can sue on it?",options:["Yes","No — a stranger to the contract still cannot sue on it","Yes, for written contracts","Only where the consideration came from the stranger"],answer:1,why:"Consideration may come from a stranger. Who may sue is a separate question, and the answer is privity.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 2(d); Kepong Prospecting Ltd v Schmidt [1968] 1 MLJ 170 (PC)"},{id:"qcs3",q:"What does section 26(a) require?",options:["Writing and registration, natural love and affection, and parties in a near relation","Writing alone","Registration alone","Natural love and affection alone"],answer:0,why:"Writing on its own is not enough. This is the limb most often stated only halfway.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 26(a)"},{id:"qcs4",q:"What does Explanation 2 to section 26 say?",options:["Consideration must be adequate","Inadequate consideration does not make an agreement void, but it may bear on free consent","Inadequate consideration is no consideration at all","Adequacy is entirely for the parties"],answer:1,why:"It is the bridge between consideration and the provisions on free consent.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 26, Explanation 2"},{id:"qcs5",q:"A promise is made in return for a past voluntary act done for the promisor. Which limb saves it?",options:["Section 26(a)","Section 26(c)","Section 26(b)","None — it fails"],answer:2,why:"The act must have been voluntary and done for the promisor. That is the safe route where nobody asked for it.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 26(b)"}]},{id:"l-terms",moduleId:"m06-contract",title:"Terms and representations: what actually got into the contract",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Not everything said before signing becomes part of the contract. Which side of that line a statement falls on decides whether you sue for breach or attack the agreement itself.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 (Act 136) ss 14, 19(1) and 74; Civil Law Act 1956 s 3. The term and representation distinction, and the routes by which a term is implied, are received common law rather than statute.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for the statutory implied terms in a sale of goods on purpose; confirm it in the current Sale of Goods Act 1957 as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. The tests separating a term from a representation are case law, and no section number is given for them either. Confirm the position in the current Contracts Act 1950 as published by the Attorney General's Chambers, and record the date you checked. Read ss 14, 19(1) and 74 in a current reprint before quoting the wording above.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"contracts-act-1950",where:"section 14 for what spoils consent, section 19(1) for the choice a misrepresentation gives, and section 74 for the measure of damages"},{statuteId:"civil-law-act-1956",where:"section 3, the authority for applying the received common law on terms and representations here at all"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-terms-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"The salesman said it and the paper did not, and that gap decides which claim Faizal has.",h:"Two sentences, one car",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Faizal buys a used Proton from a dealer in Ipoh for RM28,000. The salesman tells him the engine was rebuilt last year. The signed agreement says nothing about the engine."},{t:"p",text:"Three months later the engine seizes. It was never rebuilt. Faizal wants his money back."},{t:"p",text:"Everything turns on one question. Was that sentence part of the contract? Or was it only something said to get him to sign?"},{t:"p",text:"A **term** is a promise inside the contract. A **representation** is a statement of fact that led you into it. Both were spoken aloud. Only one was promised."},{t:"rule",text:"The law separates a promise that forms part of the agreement from a statement of fact that merely induced a party to enter into it. That distinction comes from the received common law, which applies in Malaysia subject to local circumstances and to any written law.",source:"General principle of contract law as received under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 3."},{t:"p",text:"The Contracts Act 1950 does not sort statements for you. It says what a contract needs. It leaves this question to the case law."},{t:"p",text:"That is why the topic feels different from formation and consideration. There you reach for a section. Here you reach for the facts, and argue."}]},{id:"sec-l-terms-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"No single test settles it: timing, importance, special knowledge and writing all pull at once.",h:"Four things a court weighs",body:[{t:"p",text:"There is no single test. A court weighs several things together, and the weight shifts with the facts."},{t:"table",caption:"What pushes a spoken statement towards being a term",columns:["What the court weighs","Points to a term","Points to a representation"],rows:[["Timing","Said at or close to the moment of signing","Said weeks earlier, with much talk in between"],["Importance","The buyer showed he would not sign without it","Nobody treated it as decisive"],["Special knowledge","The speaker knew the subject and the listener did not","The listener knew more than the speaker"],["Put in writing","The statement went into the signed document","The document stayed silent about it"]],alt:"Four things a court weighs. Timing: said at signing points to a term, said weeks earlier points to a representation. Importance: a buyer who showed he would not sign without it points to a term, and general talk points away. Special knowledge: a speaker who knew the subject and a listener who did not points to a term. Writing: a statement put into the signed document points to a term, and a silent document points away from one."},{t:"p",text:"The last row is the strongest of the four. It is still not conclusive. A signed document that stays silent can be attacked as an incomplete record of the deal."},{t:"p",text:"Notice what the whole list is doing. It asks who was in a position to know, and who was relying on whom."},{t:"example",text:"Faizal knows nothing about engines. The salesman services these cars every week. Faizal asked twice about the engine before he signed. Three of the four pointers favour a term, even though the paper says nothing."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not settle this by asking whether the statement mattered to the buyer. Almost every statement did. The question is what the two of them showed each other at the time."}]},{id:"sec-l-terms-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Some terms are in the contract without being spoken, put there by fact, by statute or by trade custom.",h:"Terms nobody wrote down",body:[{t:"p",text:"Not every term is written or spoken. Some are read into the contract by law."},{t:"p",text:"There are three ordinary routes in, and they work differently."},{t:"list",items:["**By fact.** The term is so obvious that both sides would have agreed at once had anyone raised it. Courts use this sparingly, and only to make the contract work.","**By statute.** Parliament writes terms into certain contracts. Sale of goods and hire purchase are the familiar examples, and those terms are harder to bargain away.","**By custom.** A settled practice, well known in a particular trade, can be read into an agreement made in that trade."]},{t:"p",text:"A term implied by fact is the hardest to win. You are asking a court to add words the parties never used."},{t:"example",text:"Faizal's agreement says nothing about the car being fit to drive. He may not need it to. Terms about goods sold can be implied into a contract of sale by statute, whatever the paper says."},{t:"caution",text:"Look those statutory terms up before you cite them. They live in the Sale of Goods Act 1957, not in the Contracts Act 1950, and this lesson gives no section number for them."},{t:"p",text:"So the map has three layers. What the parties wrote, what they said, and what the law puts in without being asked."}]},{id:"sec-l-terms-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A broken term is a breach measured by section 74; a false representation attacks consent under section 14.",h:"The label decides the remedy",body:[{t:"p",text:"Now the payoff. The two labels lead to two different claims."},{t:"p",text:"Say the sentence was a term. The contract stands, and one promise inside it was broken. Faizal claims the money the breach cost him."},{t:"rule",text:"When a contract has been broken, the party who suffers by the breach is entitled to receive compensation for any loss or damage caused to him which naturally arose in the usual course of things from the breach, or which the parties knew, when they made the contract, to be likely to result from the breach.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 74"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: he recovers the loss that ordinarily follows. He also recovers unusual loss the dealer knew about when the deal was struck."},{t:"p",text:"Say instead the sentence stayed outside the contract. Nothing inside it was broken. The attack is on his consent, and section 14 is the section that lists what spoils consent."},{t:"rule",text:"When consent to an agreement is caused by coercion, fraud or misrepresentation, the agreement is a contract voidable at the option of the party whose consent was so caused.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 19(1)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: a misrepresentation does not end the contract by itself. It hands Faizal a choice, and he has to make it."},{t:"p",text:"He may rescind and unwind the sale. Or he may affirm it and insist on being put in the position he would have been in, had the statement been true."},{t:"predict",prompt:"The engine sentence was typed into the signed agreement, and the engine was never rebuilt. Which route should Faizal take?",options:["Sue for breach of a term, because the promise sits inside the contract","Rescind the sale, because he was told something untrue","Neither, because he had a chance to inspect the car before buying"],answer:0,reveal:"The second one. Once the sentence is in the signed document it is a promise, not an inducement. Breaking a promise inside a contract is breach, and section 74 measures what he gets. Rescission is the route for a statement that stayed outside. Reaching for it here throws away the easier claim."},{t:"p",text:"One more step after the label. Some terms go to the root of the deal, and breaking one lets the innocent side end the contract. Breaking a minor term gives damages and nothing more."},{t:"caution",text:"Naming the statement a term wins nothing on its own. Say how serious the breach was, and say what your client can actually do about it. That is where the marks are."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-exclusion",moduleId:"m06-contract",title:"Exclusion clauses: getting in, and getting past the words",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"A clause that takes liability away has to clear three hurdles before it protects anybody. It must get into the contract, cover what actually went wrong, and survive the limits the law imposes from outside.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 (Act 136) ss 14 and 24; Civil Law Act 1956 s 3; Consumer Protection Act 1999, named without a section number. Incorporation and construction of exclusion clauses are received common law rather than statute.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for the unfair terms provisions in consumer contracts on purpose; confirm them in the current Consumer Protection Act 1999 as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. No section number is given for any statutory control of exclusion of liability either. Confirm it in the current Contracts Act 1950 as published by the Attorney General's Chambers, and record the date you checked. The incorporation and construction rules are stated without case citations on purpose. Check them against a Malaysian contract text and note the local authorities yourself.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"contracts-act-1950",where:"section 24 on an unlawful object, and section 14 for the free consent point behind a signature obtained by misleading someone"},{statuteId:"civil-law-act-1956",where:"section 3, which is why the received rules of construction apply to these clauses at all"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-exclusion-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Three hurdles stand between the operator and his clause, and most answers argue only the last one.",h:"The ticket in the car park",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Nurul parks at a car park in Johor Bahru. The machine prints a ticket. On the back, in small type, it says the operator is not liable for loss or damage howsoever caused."},{t:"p",text:"She comes back to a dented door and a missing wing mirror. The attendant points at the ticket."},{t:"p",text:"An **exclusion clause** takes away a liability that would otherwise exist. A **limitation clause** caps it at a figure instead of removing it. The analysis below fits both."},{t:"p",text:"That clause has to clear three hurdles before it helps the operator at all."},{t:"list",items:["**Did it get in?** A clause binds nobody unless it became part of the contract.","**Does it cover this?** The words have to reach the thing that actually went wrong.","**Does the law allow it?** Some clauses fail even when they are in and on point."]},{t:"p",text:"Take them in that order. Arguing that a clause is unfair is wasted work if it never got into the contract in the first place."}]},{id:"sec-l-exclusion-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Signature, notice given before the deal is struck, or a long course of dealing between the same parties.",h:"Getting in",body:[{t:"p",text:"A clause becomes part of a contract in one of three ordinary ways."},{t:"list",items:["**Signature.** A person who signs a document is normally bound by what is in it, read or not.","**Notice.** An unsigned clause binds only if reasonable steps were taken to bring it to the other side's attention. Those steps must come before or at the moment the contract is made.","**Course of dealing.** Where the same two parties have dealt on the same terms many times, those terms may carry into the next deal."]},{t:"p",text:"The timing point does the most work of the three. A clause handed over after the deal is struck has arrived too late to be part of it."},{t:"example",text:"Nurul's contract was made when she took the ticket and the barrier lifted. Words printed on the back of that ticket reached her at that same moment, or later. The operator has to show she had reasonable notice by then."},{t:"p",text:"The more unusual the clause, the more the law expects it to have been pointed out. A sweeping clause buried in small print is the hardest of all to bring in."},{t:"caution",text:"A signature is strong but it is not magic. It can be attacked where the signer was misled about what the document contained, and section 14 of the Contracts Act 1950 is where that argument starts."}]},{id:"sec-l-exclusion-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Ambiguity is read against the party relying on the clause, and excluding your own negligence needs clear words.",h:"Getting past the words",body:[{t:"p",text:"Suppose the clause got in. The next question is whether it covers what happened."},{t:"p",text:"Courts read these clauses strictly. Any real ambiguity is resolved against the party who is relying on it."},{t:"p",text:"That rule has a name worth knowing: contra proferentem. It means against the one who put the words forward."},{t:"rule",text:"An exclusion clause is construed strictly, and any ambiguity in it is resolved against the party seeking to rely on it. Clear words are required before such a clause is read as excluding liability for that party's own negligence.",source:"Received rules of construction, applied in Malaysia under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 3."},{t:"p",text:"So sweeping words like howsoever caused are weaker than they look. A court asks what the clause plainly covers, not what it could be stretched to reach."},{t:"example",text:"Say the dent came from an attendant reversing a van into Nurul's car. The operator is then asking the clause to cover his own carelessness. He needs words that say so."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A clause says the operator is not liable for any loss howsoever caused. An attendant damages the car through carelessness. Is the operator covered?",options:["Yes, because howsoever caused covers everything","No, because a clause can never cover negligence","Not without clearer words, because excluding your own negligence requires them"],answer:2,why:"General words are read strictly, and against the person relying on them. The third option overstates it, since negligence can be excluded where the clause plainly says so. The first option adopts the drafter's sweep as if it were the court's reading."}]},{id:"sec-l-exclusion-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Section 24 and the consumer legislation set an outer limit that careful drafting cannot get past.",h:"Where the law says no",body:[{t:"p",text:"The third hurdle is the law's own limit. Two sources matter here, and they work differently."},{t:"p",text:"The first is the Contracts Act itself. Where the object or the consideration of an agreement is unlawful, the agreement is void."},{t:"rule",text:"The consideration or object of an agreement is unlawful if it is forbidden by law, or would defeat any law, or is fraudulent, or involves injury to the person or property of another, or the court regards it as immoral or opposed to public policy. Every agreement of which the object or consideration is unlawful is void.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 24"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: a clause that exists to defeat a law, or that offends public policy, is not rescued by careful drafting."},{t:"p",text:"The second source is consumer protection legislation. Malaysia has a statute dealing with unfair terms in consumer contracts, and it works outside the Contracts Act."},{t:"caution",text:"This lesson gives no section number for those unfair terms provisions. Find them in the current Consumer Protection Act 1999, and write down the date you checked."},{t:"p",text:"Notice who that statute helps. It reaches consumer dealings. A contract between two businesses is usually fought on the first two hurdles instead."},{t:"p",text:"So the order matters once more. Incorporation, then construction, then control. An answer that opens on unfairness has skipped the two hurdles the operator is likeliest to fail."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-privity",moduleId:"m06-contract",title:"Privity: who can actually sue on the promise",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Consideration in Malaysia may come from a stranger, but a stranger still cannot sue on the contract. That gap catches the person who paid for work done for somebody else.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 (Act 136) ss 2(d) and 74; Civil Law Act 1956 s 3; Kepong Prospecting Ltd v Schmidt. Privity itself is received common law, not a provision of the Act.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for agency, or for a trust of a promise, on purpose; confirm it in the current Contracts Act 1950 as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. Kepong Prospecting Ltd v Schmidt is named without a law report reference on purpose, so look the report up and write the citation down yourself. Check as well whether any Malaysian statute now gives a third party a direct right to enforce a contract made for their benefit.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"contracts-act-1950",where:"section 2(d) for where consideration may come from, then section 74 for whose loss is measured"},{statuteId:"civil-law-act-1956",where:"section 3, the route by which the privity doctrine applies in Malaysia at all"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-privity-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Ganesan signed and paid, and his mother got the bad workmanship. Only one of them is in the contract.",h:"The son paid, the mother lives there",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Ganesan pays a contractor RM40,000 to rewire his mother's house in Klang. He signs the agreement. He pays every instalment himself."},{t:"p",text:"The work is bad. Sockets spark. His mother, Puan Letchumi, is the one living with it."},{t:"p",text:"She wants to sue the contractor. She has the loss, the damage and the inconvenience. What she does not have is a contract."},{t:"p",text:"That single fact is the whole problem, and it has a name."},{t:"rule",text:"Only a party to a contract may sue upon it. A person who is not a party acquires no right to enforce the promise, even where the promise was made for that person's benefit.",source:"The doctrine of privity of contract, received in Malaysia under the Civil Law Act 1956, s 3."},{t:"p",text:"**Privity of contract** means being one of the parties to the deal. Letchumi is not, however plainly the work was meant for her."}]},{id:"sec-l-privity-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Who supplied the consideration and who may sue are two different questions with two different answers.",h:"Why section 2(d) does not rescue her",body:[{t:"p",text:"Malaysian consideration is wider than the English kind. Section 2(d) of the Contracts Act 1950 says so in plain words."},{t:"rule",text:"When, at the desire of the promisor, the promisee or any other person has done or abstained from doing, or does or abstains from doing, or promises to do or to abstain from doing, something, such act or abstinence or promise is called a consideration for the promise.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 2(d)"},{t:"p",text:"Read the phrase the promisee or any other person. Consideration may move from somebody who is not the promisee at all."},{t:"p",text:"It is tempting to run that straight into privity. If a stranger's money can be the price, why can a stranger not sue?"},{t:"p",text:"Because the section answers a different question. It says what counts as consideration. It says nothing about who may bring the action."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Letchumi's neighbour pays one instalment as a favour. Does that give the neighbour a claim against the contractor?",options:["Yes, because she supplied consideration under section 2(d)","No, because supplying consideration is not the same as being a party","Only if the contractor knew where the money had come from"],answer:1,reveal:"The second one. Section 2(d) widens what can count as the price of a promise. It does not widen the list of people who may sue on it. If you picked the first answer, you have made the mistake this topic is built around. Keep the two questions apart and the case law stays straight."},{t:"p",text:"Kepong Prospecting Ltd v Schmidt is the decision usually cited here. It gets cited for consideration and for privity, and running the two together is the standard error."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not write that Malaysia has abolished privity. It has not. Section 2(d) changed where consideration may come from, and it changed nothing else."}]},{id:"sec-l-privity-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Agency, a trust of the promise, assignment, statute and a collateral contract each get the third party in by a different door.",h:"The ways around it",body:[{t:"p",text:"The rule is real, and so are the routes around it. None of them breaks privity. Each one finds the third party a contract of their own, or a right of some other kind."},{t:"list",items:["**Agency.** If Ganesan contracted as his mother's agent, she is the real party and the difficulty disappears.","**Trust of a promise.** Where the promisee holds the benefit of the promise on trust for the third party, that party can be brought before the court.","**Assignment.** A contracting party may transfer the benefit of the contract to somebody else, who then sues in their own name.","**Statute.** Some statutes give a named person a direct claim. Insurance is the familiar example.","**Collateral contract.** A separate small contract between the promisor and the third party, supported by its own consideration."]},{t:"p",text:"Test agency first. It is often true on the facts and rarely argued. Ask who was named, and ask who was really doing the dealing."},{t:"example",text:"Say Letchumi asked her son to arrange the rewiring, gave him the money and told him to sign for her. The argument is agency. She is then a party, and privity never bites at all."},{t:"caution",text:"This lesson gives no section numbers for agency. The Contracts Act 1950 deals with agency in a Part of its own, and that numbering must be checked before you cite it."},{t:"p",text:"Tort is the other exit, and it is not an exception to privity. A person injured by careless work may have a claim that never depended on a contract."}]},{id:"sec-l-privity-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Ganesan can sue, but section 74 measures the loss caused to him, and the loss was his mother's.",h:"What the party with the contract can recover",body:[{t:"p",text:"Turn the problem around. Ganesan has the contract, so he can sue. His trouble is the measure of what he gets."},{t:"rule",text:"When a contract has been broken, the party who suffers by the breach is entitled to receive compensation for any loss or damage caused to him which naturally arose in the usual course of things from the breach, or which the parties knew, when they made the contract, to be likely to result from the breach.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 74"},{t:"p",text:"Read the words caused to him. The section measures the loss of the party who suffers by the breach."},{t:"p",text:"The contractor will say Ganesan lost nothing. He does not live in the house. The sparking sockets are not his to complain of."},{t:"p",text:"The answer runs through what Ganesan actually bargained for. He paid for a sound installation in that house, and he did not get one."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Letchumi sues the contractor in her own name over the wiring. What is the first answer she will meet?",options:["That the work was reasonable in the circumstances","That she is not a party to the contract and cannot sue on it","That she paid nothing towards the price"],answer:1,why:"Privity is the threshold point and it is taken first. The third option confuses consideration with standing, because under section 2(d) consideration can come from anyone and still not make her a party. The first option is the trial she never reaches."},{t:"p",text:"So the practical lesson here is about drafting, not litigation. Name the person who will suffer the loss as a party, and the problem never arises."},{t:"caution",text:"Check whether Parliament has since legislated on the rights of third parties. Several jurisdictions have done so, and a statute of that kind would move the starting point of this lesson."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-illegality",moduleId:"m06-contract",title:"Unlawful object: how section 24 kills an agreement",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Section 24 lists five ways the purpose or the price of a deal can be unlawful. Any one of them makes the whole agreement void, and getting your money back afterwards is a separate fight.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 (Act 136) ss 10(1), 24 and 66. The provisions on a partly unlawful agreement and on restraint of trade are referred to without section numbers on purpose.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for a partly unlawful agreement, or for agreements in restraint of trade, on purpose; confirm it in the current Contracts Act 1950 as published by the Attorney General's Chambers and record the date you checked. Sections 10(1), 24 and 66 are given in shortened form, so read the full text before relying on the wording. The point about section 66 and an agreement discovered to be void is an argument, not a settled rule. It needs current Malaysian authority before you put it in an answer.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"contracts-act-1950",where:"section 24 read slowly, one limb at a time, then section 66 on restoration, then section 10(1) for where the lawful object requirement comes from"}],glossaryDense:"The section 24 rule block recites the five heads that make an object unlawful, and the lesson is about those heads. A reader has to be able to run down the list and test their facts against each one, which is exactly what flattening it into prose would take away.",sections:[{id:"sec-l-illegality-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Zulkifli's agreement was written, signed and witnessed, and it was worth nothing the moment it was made.",h:"Paying a man to arrange a licence",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Zulkifli wants a licence for a food stall. A man tells him he knows the right officer. For RM15,000 he will arrange it."},{t:"p",text:"They write it down. Zulkifli pays RM8,000 up front. No licence ever arrives."},{t:"p",text:"He wants the licence, or the RM8,000 back. He has a signed agreement, a witness and a receipt."},{t:"p",text:"None of that helps him. A court will not enforce this agreement, and the reason has nothing to do with how it was made."},{t:"rule",text:"All agreements are contracts if they are made by the free consent of parties competent to contract, for a lawful consideration and with a lawful object, and are not hereby expressly declared to be void.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 10(1)"},{t:"p",text:"Read the phrase lawful object. Formation, consent and consideration can all be perfect. If the object is unlawful the agreement fails anyway."},{t:"p",text:"This is the one contract defect that a careful drafter cannot design around. It is about what the parties are doing, not how they wrote it."}]},{id:"sec-l-illegality-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Forbidden by law, defeating a law, fraudulent, injurious, or against public policy — one limb is enough.",h:"The five ways to be unlawful",body:[{t:"p",text:"Section 24 is a list. Read it as five separate limbs. An argument in a real case is about which limb is engaged."},{t:"rule",text:"The consideration or object of an agreement is unlawful if it is forbidden by law, or is of such a nature that, if permitted, it would defeat any law, or is fraudulent, or involves or implies injury to the person or property of another, or the court regards it as immoral or opposed to public policy. Every agreement of which the object or consideration is unlawful is void.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 24"},{t:"table",caption:"The five limbs of section 24, with the everyday shape of each",columns:["Limb","What it catches"],rows:[["Forbidden by law","A deal to do the very thing a statute makes an offence"],["Would defeat any law","A deal lawful on its face, arranged to get around a statute"],["Fraudulent","A deal whose purpose is to cheat somebody, inside the deal or outside it"],["Injury to person or property","A deal to hurt somebody, or to damage what belongs to them"],["Immoral or against public policy","A deal the court will not lend its help to, such as one corrupting a public duty"]],alt:"The five limbs of section 24. Forbidden by law catches a deal to do the very thing a statute makes an offence. Defeating a law catches a deal that is lawful on its face but arranged to get around a statute. Fraudulent catches a deal whose purpose is to cheat. Injury to person or property catches a deal to hurt someone or damage their belongings. Immoral or against public policy catches a deal the court will not help, such as one corrupting a public duty."},{t:"p",text:"Zulkifli's agreement fits more than one limb. Paying for the exercise of a public duty corrupts it, and the second limb reaches an arrangement built to work around a licensing scheme."},{t:"p",text:"Name the limb. An answer that says the deal is illegal, without saying which limb, has not started."},{t:"caution",text:"Public policy is the widest limb and the most abused. It is not a place to put an argument that would not fit anywhere else, and a court will want the policy named."}]},{id:"sec-l-illegality-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Void means there was never a contract, so the claim for the RM8,000 has to come from somewhere else.",h:"Void, and getting the money back",body:[{t:"p",text:"Section 24 makes the agreement void. Void means it had no legal effect from the very start."},{t:"p",text:"So Zulkifli cannot sue on it. There is no promise to enforce, and no breach to complain of."},{t:"p",text:"His RM8,000 is a separate question. The Act deals with restoring what somebody received under an agreement that turns out to be void."},{t:"rule",text:"When an agreement is discovered to be void, or when a contract becomes void, any person who has received any advantage under the agreement or contract is bound to restore it, or to make compensation for it, to the person from whom he received it.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 66"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: money paid under a void agreement normally has to go back."},{t:"p",text:"Now read the opening words again. Section 66 speaks of an agreement discovered to be void."},{t:"p",text:"Zulkifli knew from the beginning what he was paying for. The other side will argue that nothing was discovered, because nothing had been hidden from him."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Zulkifli sues for his RM8,000 back under section 66. What is the strongest answer against him?",options:["That the agreement was in writing, so he is bound by its terms","That section 66 applies only to dealings in land","That he knew the object was unlawful, so nothing was discovered to be void"],answer:2,reveal:"The second. Section 66 turns on an agreement discovered to be void, and a party who knew all along has trouble with that word. Do not assert this as a settled rule. It is the argument you have to meet, and it needs current authority. The first option gets it backwards, because writing does not rescue an unlawful object."},{t:"caution",text:"This is the contested part of the topic. Do not write that a party to an unlawful deal always recovers, or never does. Set out the argument on both sides and say which you prefer."}]},{id:"sec-l-illegality-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Part of an agreement can be unlawful without killing all of it, and restraint of trade has a provision of its own.",h:"Where the section stops",body:[{t:"p",text:"Two things sit just outside section 24. Both turn up in the same problem questions."},{t:"p",text:"The first is an agreement that is only partly unlawful. One clause offends, and the rest is ordinary commerce."},{t:"p",text:"The Contracts Act deals with that case in a provision of its own. Whether the good part survives depends on whether the unlawful part can be cut out cleanly."},{t:"caution",text:"This lesson gives no section number for the partly unlawful agreement, or for agreements in restraint of trade. Both have their own provisions, and both numbers must be checked in the current text before you cite them."},{t:"p",text:"The second is restraint of trade. That means a promise not to compete, not to work in a trade, or not to deal with certain customers."},{t:"p",text:"Malaysia treats these by statute, and the statutory position is not the English one. Do not import the English test of reasonableness without reading the Act."},{t:"example",text:"A shop in Melaka is sold, and the seller promises not to open a similar shop nearby. That promise is dealt with by the Act's own provision, which carries its own exceptions. It is not settled by asking whether the promise was reasonable."},{t:"p",text:"So an illegality answer ends with two checks. Is the whole agreement gone, or only part of it? And is the clause you are attacking one the Act treats separately?"},{t:"caution",text:"Public policy moves. A judgment from thirty years ago may not describe what offends it today. This is a topic where the date of your authority matters."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-vitiating",moduleId:"m06-contract",title:"When an agreement can be undone: the vitiating factors",minutes:30,summary:"Free consent is defined by the five things that spoil it. Each has its own section in the Contracts Act 1950, and they do not all have the same consequence.",plants:["ms-vit-01","ms-vit-02","ms-vit-03","ms-vit-04","ms-vit-05"],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"cheong-contract-law-malaysia",where:"the chapters on free consent and on void and voidable agreements"}],source:"Contracts Act 1950 ss 10, 11, 14-24, 66; Age of Majority Act 1971.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Check each section against the current consolidated text of Act 136 — the numbering above is the traditional numbering and should be confirmed. The Malaysian position on a minor's contracts, in particular, should be checked against current authority rather than assumed from the English rules.",plate:"broken-seal",plateCaption:"A deed with the seal broken. Voidable means the agreement stands until the party entitled to avoid it says otherwise.",readMinutes:11,glossaryDense:"Five vitiating factors, each with a statutory definition narrower than the everyday word, in one lesson. The marks are the point of it.",sections:[{id:"sec-l-vitiating-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A contract needs free consent, and section 14 defines that by what removes it.",h:"Consent that was not free",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Aini signs a paper agreeing to sell her land for RM30,000. She signs because the buyer told her the land floods every year and is worth nothing. It does not flood. She agreed, but she did not agree with her eyes open."},{t:"p",text:"The law has a list of the things that spoil consent. It is a closed list, and it is in the Contracts Act 1950. Start with what a contract needs in the first place."},{t:"rule",text:"An agreement is a contract if it is made by the free consent of parties competent to contract, for a lawful consideration and with a lawful object, and is not expressly declared to be void.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 10(1)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: four things are needed. Consent that is free, parties old enough and of sound mind, a lawful price and a lawful purpose, and no provision declaring the agreement void."},{t:"p",text:"Consideration is the price of a promise — what each side gives in exchange for what it gets. That leaves the phrase in the middle, free consent, which the next section defines."},{t:"rule",text:"Consent is said to be free when it is not caused by coercion, undue influence, fraud, misrepresentation, or mistake.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 14"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: consent is free unless one of five named things caused it. Lawyers call those five the **vitiating factors**, from a word that means to spoil or impair."},{t:"caution",text:"Section 14 is a closed list, and ss 15 to 23 supply the statutory definitions. An answer that reasons from English duress, or from English misrepresentation, has started in the wrong place. It usually gets the consequence wrong as well."}]},{id:"sec-l-vitiating-1",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Coercion, undue influence, fraud, misrepresentation, mistake — each with a statutory definition narrower than the everyday word.",h:"The five, section by section",body:[{t:"list",items:["**Coercion (s 15)** — committing or threatening to commit an act forbidden by the Penal Code. Or unlawfully detaining property, or threatening to detain it, to the prejudice of any person. It must be done with the intention of causing a person to enter into the agreement. Notice how much narrower that is than 'pressure'.","**Undue influence (s 16)** — the relations between the parties are such that one can dominate the will of the other, and he uses that position to obtain an unfair advantage. Section 16(2) gives instances of such a position. Where the transaction appears unconscionable, s 16(3) puts it on the dominant party to show the contract was not induced by undue influence.","**Fraud (s 17)** — suggesting a fact is true when you do not believe it to be true. Also active concealment, and a promise made with no intention of performing it. Mere silence is not fraud, unless there is a duty to speak, or the silence is in itself equivalent to speech.","**Misrepresentation (s 18)** — a positive assertion, not warranted by the information the maker has, of something untrue that he believes to be true. It also covers certain breaches of duty, and causing a party to make a mistake about the subject matter. The difference from fraud is honesty.","**Mistake (ss 21 to 23)** — a mistake by both parties as to a matter of fact essential to the agreement makes it void. A mistake as to a law in force in Malaysia does not."]},{t:"example",text:"Pak Hamid is eighty and unwell. His nephew handles his bank account, his papers and his medicine. The nephew has him sign a transfer of the house for RM1. The relationship is the point: the nephew was in a position to dominate his uncle's will, and the transaction appears unconscionable. Section 16(3) then puts it on the nephew to show the transfer was not induced by undue influence."},{t:"caution",text:"Unconscionable is not the same as unwise. People are entitled to make bad bargains, and the courts do not rewrite them. The word points at a transaction so one-sided that it calls for an explanation."}]},{id:"sec-l-vitiating-2",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Coercion, fraud and misrepresentation make an agreement voidable; a shared mistake of essential fact makes it void.",h:"Void and voidable",body:[{t:"p",text:"Two words do a great deal of work here and they are easy to confuse."},{t:"p",text:"**Void** means the agreement had no legal effect from the beginning. There is nothing there to keep. **Voidable** means the agreement is valid and binding until the innocent party elects to undo it."},{t:"p",text:"Which one you get depends on which vitiating factor you are dealing with."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Aini signed because the buyer told her the land floods. It does not flood. Is there a contract at all?",options:["No — a lie wipes the agreement out from the start","Yes, and the only thing she can ask for is money","Yes, and it binds her until she chooses to undo it"],answer:2,reveal:"There is a contract. Coercion, fraud and misrepresentation make an agreement voidable rather than void, so it stands until the wronged party takes it down. If you picked the first answer, that instinct is not wasted. It is right about a mistake made by both parties, which section 21 does make void."},{t:"rule",text:"When consent to an agreement is caused by coercion, fraud or misrepresentation, the agreement is a contract voidable at the option of the party whose consent was so caused.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 19(1)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: coercion, fraud and misrepresentation give the wronged party a choice. The contract stands unless and until that party takes it down."},{t:"p",text:"She may rescind it — unwind it and put both sides back where they started. Or she may insist that the contract be performed, and that she be put in the position she would have been in if what she was told had been true."},{t:"p",text:"The right to rescind can be lost. It goes if she affirms the contract after learning the truth, if she waits too long, or if restitution has become impossible."},{t:"p",text:"A mistake by both parties about an essential fact is a different case. Section 21 makes that agreement void. There is no election to make, because there is nothing to affirm."},{t:"p",text:"Section 66 then deals with restoration. A person who has received an advantage under an agreement discovered to be void must restore it, or make compensation for it. The same goes for a contract that becomes void. It does the work an English lawyer would look for in restitution."},{t:"example",text:"Aini learns that the flood story was false. She may rescind, hand back the price and take her land. Or she may hold the buyer to the sale on the footing that what he said was true. What she may not do is wait two years, spend the money, and then rescind."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"Which factor you have decides which word you get",caption:"The practical difference is the election. Voidable leaves the choice with the wronged party; void leaves nobody anything to elect about.",alt:"A question — which vitiating factor caused the consent? — branching two ways. Coercion, fraud or misrepresentation under section 19: the agreement is voidable, and the wronged party may rescind or may affirm and insist on performance. A mistake by both parties as to an essential fact falls under section 21. That agreement is void from the start. There is no election to make, and section 66 handles restoration.",question:"Which factor caused the consent?",branches:[{cond:"coercion, fraud, misrepresentation",label:"Voidable (s 19)",note:"she elects: rescind, or affirm and be put in the true position"},{cond:"mutual mistake of essential fact",label:"Void (s 21)",note:"no election; s 66 restoration"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-vitiating-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Capacity under s 11, and an unlawful object or consideration under s 24, are separate ways to fail.",h:"Capacity, and lawful objects",body:[{t:"p",text:"Two more requirements sit alongside free consent, and both come from section 10."},{t:"p",text:"Section 11 makes a person competent to contract if he is of the age of majority, of sound mind, and not disqualified from contracting by any law. The age of majority is eighteen, under the Age of Majority Act 1971."},{t:"caution",text:"What happens to a minor's agreement in Malaysia is governed by our own statutes and our own authorities. Do not assume the English infancy rules. Check current Malaysian authority instead."},{t:"p",text:"The other requirement is that what the parties are doing must be lawful. Section 24 says when it is not."},{t:"rule",text:"The consideration or object of an agreement is unlawful if it is forbidden by law, or would defeat any law, or is fraudulent, or involves injury to person or property, or the court regards it as immoral or opposed to public policy; every agreement of which the object or consideration is unlawful is void.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 24"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: if the purpose or the price of the deal falls into any of those categories, the whole agreement is void."},{t:"example",text:"Two people agree that one will pay the other RM5,000 to withdraw a police report. Whatever they wrote down, that is not a contract any court will enforce. Section 24 makes it void."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not reach for 'illegality' as one big general doctrine. Section 24 lists the categories, and s 25 deals with an agreement that is only partly unlawful. The argument in a real case is about which limb of s 24 is engaged."}]},{id:"sec-l-vitiating-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Name the factor and its section, state the definition, apply it both ways, then say void or voidable.",h:"How to work through it",body:[{t:"p",text:"Name the vitiating factor and its section. State the statutory definition. Apply it to the facts, and argue it both ways where it is arguable."},{t:"p",text:"Then state the consequence — void or voidable — and what follows from that for the remedy your client actually wants. Answers lose their footing in that last step far more often than the first."},{t:"steps",title:"The order to work in",items:[{h:"Name the factor, and its section.",text:"Coercion is s 15, undue influence s 16, fraud s 17, misrepresentation s 18, mistake ss 21 to 23. The section number is part of the answer."},{h:"State the statutory definition.",text:"Quote the Act, not an English formulation of the same idea. The definitions here are statutory and they do not match the English doctrines."},{h:"Apply it to the facts.",text:"Argue it both ways wherever it is genuinely arguable. Most of the marks live in this step."},{h:"State the consequence.",text:"Void, or voidable. This follows from which factor you named, not from how serious the conduct feels."},{h:"Say what follows for the remedy.",text:"Rescission, affirmation with compensation, or restoration under s 66. Answers lose their footing when they stop at the label."}]}]}],quiz:[{id:"qvt1",q:"Both parties are mistaken about a fact essential to their agreement. What is the effect?",options:["The agreement is voidable","The agreement is valid","The agreement is unenforceable but not void","The agreement is void"],answer:3,why:"Section 21 makes it void, so there is nothing to affirm. Voidability under s 19 belongs to coercion, fraud and misrepresentation.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 ss 21 and 19"},{id:"qvt2",q:"Which section defines coercion, and what does it require?",options:["Section 17 — a false suggestion of fact","Section 15 — an act forbidden by the Penal Code, or unlawful detention of property","Section 16 — domination of the will","Section 14 — any improper pressure"],answer:1,why:"The statutory definition is far narrower than 'pressure', and that is where English duress reasoning goes wrong here.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 15"},{id:"qvt3",q:"When does staying silent amount to fraud under s 17?",options:["Always, if a material fact is withheld","Never","Where there is a duty to speak, or the silence is equivalent to speech","Only in contracts of insurance"],answer:2,why:"The explanation to s 17 sets out both limbs. Silence on its own is not fraud.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 17"},{id:"qvt4",q:"What separates misrepresentation under s 18 from fraud under s 17?",options:["The maker believes what he says is true","The statement is about the future","It concerns a third party","It is made in writing"],answer:0,why:"Section 18 covers an assertion not warranted by the information the maker has, though he believes it true.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 ss 17 and 18"},{id:"qvt5",q:"Someone has received a benefit under an agreement discovered to be void. Which section makes him give it back?",options:["Section 24","Section 19","Section 66","Section 74"],answer:2,why:"Section 66 does the work that restitution does in other systems.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 66"},{id:"qvt6",q:"What is the age of majority for capacity to contract?",options:["Twenty-one","Eighteen","Sixteen","Depends on the State"],answer:1,why:"Eighteen, under the Age of Majority Act 1971, read with s 11 of the Contracts Act 1950.",source:"Age of Majority Act 1971; Contracts Act 1950 s 11"}]},{id:"l-remedies",moduleId:"m06-contract",title:"Breach, damages, and the trouble with an agreed sum",minutes:30,summary:"Section 74 measures the compensation. Section 75 governs a sum the parties named in advance, and the law on that has moved in Malaysia within the last decade.",plants:["ms-rem-01","ms-rem-02","ms-rem-03","ms-rem-04","ms-rem-05"],prepares:["pa-remedies-01"],reading:[{bookId:"cheong-contract-law-malaysia",where:"the chapters on discharge and remedies"},{bookId:"sinnadurai-law-of-contract",where:"for the fuller treatment of s 75 and the Selva Kumar line"}],source:"Contracts Act 1950 ss 40, 56, 57, 74, 75; Specific Relief Act 1950 ss 11 and 20; Hadley v Baxendale (1854) 9 Exch 341; Selva Kumar a/l Murugiah v Thiagarajah a/l Retnasamy [1995] 1 MLJ 817 (FC); Cubic Electronics Sdn Bhd v Mars Telecommunications Sdn Bhd [2019] 6 MLJ 15 (FC), also reported at [2019] 2 CLJ 723; Cavendish Square Holding BV v Talal El Makdessi [2015] UKSC 67.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Every citation here must be checked in a report you can hold, and you must check whether any Federal Court decision after Cubic Electronics has qualified it. Note on that citation: the corpus previously gave it as [2019] 2 MLJ 806, which was wrong. It is [2019] 6 MLJ 15, also reported at [2019] 2 CLJ 723 and [2019] 2 MLRA 83. The s 75 position has changed once and could change again; this is the paragraph in the whole module most likely to go stale.",plate:"ledger",plateCaption:"The clause against the loss. Section 75 asks what is reasonable, and what counts as reasonable moved once already.",readMinutes:10,sections:[{id:"sec-l-remedies-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Section 40 lets the innocent party end the contract — but that right is an election and can be lost.",h:"When one side walks away",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Lim pays a RM5,000 deposit for a second-hand van. A week before delivery the seller says he has sold it to somebody else. What can Lim do?"},{t:"p",text:"He has two paths open and he has to pick one. He can treat the contract as over and claim what the breach cost him. Or he can hold the seller to it and keep pressing for the van."},{t:"rule",text:"When a party to a contract has refused to perform, or disabled himself from performing, his promise in its entirety, the promisee may put an end to the contract, unless he has signified by words or conduct his acquiescence in its continuance.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 40"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: if the other side refuses to perform at all, you may call the contract off. You lose that right if you have shown that you want the contract to carry on."},{t:"p",text:"The promisee is the person the promise was made to. Ending the contract is an election — a choice, made once, and capable of being lost."},{t:"p",text:"A party who keeps pressing for performance after a repudiation may be held to have affirmed the contract. Affirmation leaves him with damages only."},{t:"p",text:"Time is dealt with by ss 56 and 57. Whether time is of the essence is a question of the parties' intention. A stipulation as to time in a sale of goods or of land is not automatically essential."}]},{id:"sec-l-remedies-1",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Section 74 is the rule in Hadley v Baxendale, enacted: loss arising naturally, or within contemplation.",h:"How much money: section 74",body:[{t:"p",text:"Damages are meant to put the injured party where performance would have put him. Not better off, and not worse off. Section 74 is where that measure comes from."},{t:"rule",text:"When a contract has been broken, the party who suffers by the breach is entitled to receive compensation for any loss or damage caused to him which naturally arose in the usual course of things from the breach, or which the parties knew, when they made the contract, to be likely to result from the breach. Such compensation is not to be given for any remote and indirect loss or damage.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 74(1) and (2)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: you can recover the loss that would ordinarily follow from such a breach. You can also recover unusual loss, but only if the other side knew about the risk when the contract was made. Loss that is remote is not recoverable at all."},{t:"p",text:"Those two limbs will look familiar. This is the rule in *Hadley v Baxendale* (1854) 9 Exch 341, an English Exchequer decision, put into statute. That matters for how you argue it: the starting point is the statutory words, with the English cases as illustration rather than as the source of the rule."},{t:"example",text:"Lim wanted the van for deliveries. Hiring a replacement costs him RM100 a day, and that is ordinary loss under the first limb. Now suppose that when they struck the deal he told the seller he needed the van for one large job worth RM40,000. That loss may fall under the second limb, because the seller knew of it."},{t:"p",text:"The explanation to section 74 adds a practical point. In estimating the loss, the means available for remedying the inconvenience caused by the non-performance must be taken into account. That is the statutory foothold for mitigation."},{t:"example",text:"Mitigation is the duty to take reasonable steps to limit your own loss. If a similar van could be hired down the road, Lim cannot sit still for three months and send the seller the bill."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Lim never mentioned the RM40,000 job when the deal was struck. Can he recover that loss?",options:["No — unusual loss counts only if the seller knew of the risk when the contract was made","Yes — it followed from the breach","Yes, if he tells the seller as soon as the van fails to arrive"],answer:0,why:"The second limb of section 74 turns on what the parties knew when they made the contract. It does not turn on what the seller learns afterwards. The trap is timing. The everyday cost of hiring a replacement is still recoverable under the first limb."}]},{id:"sec-l-remedies-2",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Section 75 governs a sum named in the contract, and what counts as reasonable moved with Cubic Electronics.",anchor:"fig-l-remedies-0",h:"A sum named in the contract",body:[{t:"p",text:"Many contracts name a figure in advance. 'If you are late, you pay RM500 for every day.' Is that figure simply payable when the day comes?"},{t:"predict",prompt:"The contract says RM500 for every day the van is late. It arrives six days late. Is the seller simply liable for RM3,000?",options:["Yes — both sides agreed that figure in advance","Only reasonable compensation, and never more than RM3,000","Nothing, because a fixed sum like that is a penalty"],answer:1,reveal:"The named figure is a ceiling, not a bill. Section 75 gives reasonable compensation up to the sum named, so what Lim actually recovers still has to be justified. If you picked the first answer, you read the clause the way the parties wrote it, and in some countries that is what you would get."},{t:"rule",text:"When a contract has been broken, if a sum is named in the contract as the amount to be paid in case of such breach, the party complaining of the breach is entitled to receive from the party who has broken the contract reasonable compensation not exceeding the amount so named.",source:"Contracts Act 1950, s 75"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the named figure is a ceiling, not an automatic entitlement. What is actually paid is reasonable compensation, up to that figure."},{t:"p",text:"A plaintiff is the person who brings the case. For a long period the Malaysian position on what a plaintiff had to show came from *Selva Kumar a/l Murugiah v Thiagarajah a/l Retnasamy* [1995] 1 MLJ 817 (FC)."},{t:"p",text:"He had to prove actual loss, and he recovered only what he proved, subject to the named sum as a ceiling. The named sum was in effect a cap and nothing more."},{t:"p",text:"That was revisited in *Cubic Electronics Sdn Bhd (in liquidation) v Mars Telecommunications Sdn Bhd* [2019] 2 CLJ 723 (FC). The Federal Court reformulated the approach, drawing on the UK Supreme Court decision in *Cavendish Square Holding BV v Talal El Makdessi* [2015] UKSC 67."},{t:"p",text:"Attention now goes to two questions. Does the clause protect a legitimate interest? And is the sum out of all proportion to that interest?"},{t:"caution",text:"This is the single most important 'check before you rely on it' point in the contract module. State the sequence, name both cases, and check whether anything has been decided since. An answer that gives only *Selva Kumar* is out of date, and one that gives only *Cubic Electronics* has not shown it understands what changed."},{t:"diagram",kind:"timeline",title:"Section 75 has moved once, and could again",caption:"An answer giving only one of these two has not shown that it knows the position changed. Check whether anything has been decided since.",alt:"Three points. In 1950 the Contracts Act enacted section 75: reasonable compensation, not exceeding the sum named. In 1995 Selva Kumar held that a plaintiff must prove actual loss, with the named sum as a ceiling. In 2019 Cubic Electronics reformulated the approach around legitimate interest and proportionality to it.",events:[{year:"1950",label:"Contracts Act 1950, s 75 enacted",note:"reasonable compensation, not exceeding the sum named"},{year:"1995",label:"Selva Kumar v Thiagarajah (FC)",note:"a plaintiff must prove actual loss; the named sum is a ceiling"},{year:"2019",label:"Cubic Electronics v Mars (FC)",note:"reformulated: legitimate interest, and proportionality to it",tone:"correct"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-remedies-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Where damages cannot do the job, the Specific Relief Act 1950 governs — not the Contracts Act.",h:"When money is not enough",body:[{t:"p",text:"Some things money cannot replace. A particular piece of land is the standard example, because no two lots are the same."},{t:"p",text:"Damages are the primary remedy. Specific performance — an order compelling actual performance rather than damages — is discretionary, and it comes from a separate statute, the Specific Relief Act 1950."},{t:"p",text:"Section 11 of that Act sets out the cases in which specific performance may be granted. Section 20 sets out those in which it cannot. Two common refusals are where compensation in money is adequate relief, and where the contract would require continuous supervision."},{t:"example",text:"A buyer of a house in Cheras can usually ask the court to order the sale through. A buyer of a hundred bags of cement usually cannot. He can buy cement elsewhere and claim the difference in price."},{t:"caution",text:"The Specific Relief Act is a separate statute with its own scheme. Do not answer a specific performance question out of the Contracts Act, and do not import English equitable discretion in place of the Act's sections."}]},{id:"sec-l-remedies-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Contract, breach, termination or affirmation, measure — and only then any agreed sum.",h:"The order to work in",body:[{t:"p",text:"Establish the contract, then the breach, then whether the innocent party terminated or affirmed, then the measure of loss."},{t:"p",text:"Only then reach any agreed sum. Answers that start with the liquidated damages clause tend to skip the question whether there is a recoverable loss at all."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qrm1",q:"Section 74 of the Contracts Act 1950 puts which familiar rule into statute?",options:["The rule in Hong Kong Fir","The rule in Pinnel's Case","The rule in Hadley v Baxendale","The rule in Carlill"],answer:2,why:"The two limbs — loss arising naturally, and loss within the parties' contemplation — are the statutory form of it.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 74"},{id:"qrm2",q:"Which case reformulated the Malaysian approach to a sum named in the contract under s 75?",options:["Cubic Electronics v Mars Telecommunications","Selva Kumar v Thiagarajah","Kepong Prospecting v Schmidt","Tan Ying Hong v Tan Sian San"],answer:0,why:"The Federal Court there moved away from the strict requirement of proving actual loss, drawing on Cavendish v Makdessi.",source:"Cubic Electronics Sdn Bhd (in liquidation) v Mars Telecommunications Sdn Bhd [2019] 2 CLJ 723 (FC); Cavendish Square Holding BV v Talal El Makdessi [2015] UKSC 67"},{id:"qrm3",q:"Under s 40, what may a promisee do when the other side refuses to perform at all?",options:["Claim the named sum automatically","Seek specific performance only","Put an end to the contract, unless he has acquiesced in its continuance","Nothing until the time for performance passes"],answer:2,why:"Ending the contract is an election, and it can be lost by affirming the contract instead.",source:"Contracts Act 1950 s 40"},{id:"qrm4",q:"Which statute governs specific performance in Malaysia?",options:["The Contracts Act 1950","The Civil Law Act 1956","The Rules of Court 2012","The Specific Relief Act 1950"],answer:3,why:"The Specific Relief Act 1950 has its own scheme, in ss 11 and 20 among others. Do not answer out of the Contracts Act.",source:"Specific Relief Act 1950"},{id:"qrm5",q:"Why is specific performance a live remedy in a sale of land?",options:["Land contracts are always in writing","The National Land Code requires it","Injunctions are unavailable","Damages are treated as inadequate for land"],answer:3,why:"Whether damages are adequate is the gateway question under the Act, and for land they are treated as inadequate.",source:"Specific Relief Act 1950 ss 11 and 20"},{id:"qrm6",q:"What is the right order of analysis in a remedies question?",options:["Specific performance first","Agreed sum first, then loss","Loss first, then contract","Contract, breach, termination or affirmation, measure, then any agreed sum"],answer:3,why:"Starting at the clause skips the question whether there is a recoverable loss at all.",source:"Standard analytical order for a contract remedies question"}]}];export{e as default};
